# kept — consolidated env reference (monorepo root)
#
# This file is the SINGLE documented reference for every environment variable
# across both apps. It is committed; it contains PLACEHOLDERS ONLY — never real
# secrets. Real provisioning lands in E02 tasks 002 (Cloudflare), 003 (Railway)
# and 004 (secrets topology).
#
# WHERE EACH APP READS ITS ENV FROM:
#   - apps/web  (control plane, Next.js): copy the relevant vars below into
#               `apps/web/.env.local` (gitignored). See apps/web/.env.example
#               for the canonical, app-scoped copy used at dev/build time.
#               In deployed environments these are Railway service variables,
#               NOT a file — CI never sees them.
#   - apps/edge (data plane, Cloudflare Worker): does NOT use a .env file. Its
#               non-secret config lives in `apps/edge/wrangler.toml` (R2/KV
#               bindings, routes); secrets are set via `wrangler secret put NAME`
#               and local dev vars go in `apps/edge/.dev.vars` (gitignored).
#
# SECURITY: any var prefixed `NEXT_PUBLIC_` is shipped to the browser. Never put
# a database URL, S3 secret, or API token behind that prefix.

# =============================================================================
# apps/web — control plane (Next.js). Mirror of apps/web/.env.example.
# =============================================================================

# --- Neon Postgres / Drizzle ---
# Neon exposes two hostnames for the same database. They share a port; the
# difference is the `-pooler` infix.
#   pooled  ep-xxx-pooler.<region>.aws.neon.tech  -> runtime (PgBouncer, txn mode)
#   direct  ep-xxx.<region>.aws.neon.tech         -> migrations (DDL + advisory locks)
# Runtime MUST use pooled (hence `prepare: false` in lib/db/index.ts);
# drizzle-kit MUST use direct, or migrations fail on advisory locks.
# Prod is the Neon root branch; dev is a persistent Neon branch of it.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@ep-xxx-pooler.eu-central-1.aws.neon.tech/kept?sslmode=require
MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@ep-xxx.eu-central-1.aws.neon.tech/kept?sslmode=require

# --- Cloudflare R2 (object-storage writes from the control plane) ---
# The S3 API token MUST have Object Read & Write on BOTH buckets below.
R2_ACCOUNT_ID=your-cloudflare-account-id
R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=your-r2-access-key-id
R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=your-r2-secret-access-key
# Two buckets, selected by sites.region (@kept/shared: "auto" | "eu").
# v1 writes to AUTO (sites.region defaults to "auto"); EU is dormant until E11.
# Names are per-track — dev and prod must never share a bucket.
R2_BUCKET_AUTO=auto-kept-sites-dev
R2_BUCKET_EU=eu-kept-sites-dev

# --- Cloudflare KV (edge manifest writes via REST) ---
# Token needs Account -> Workers KV Storage -> Edit, plus Cache Purge.
CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=your-cloudflare-api-token
# Namespace "kept-manifest-dev" -> its ID. Per-track.
KV_NAMESPACE_ID=your-kv-namespace-id

# --- Cloudflare cache purge (E04; docs/edge-purge-contract.md §3) ---
# The zone whose cache the control plane purges after every manifest write
# (`POST /zones/{zone_id}/purge_cache`): kept-dev.xyz on dev, kept.host on prod.
# 32-char lowercase hex — Cloudflare dashboard, zone Overview -> API -> Zone ID,
# or `GET /zones?name=<zone>` with a Zone:Read token. Per-track; no new
# credential is needed because E02 task 004 already provisioned zone-scoped
# `Cache Purge` on both API tokens, which is what stops a dev token purging
# prod. Not itself a secret.
# UNSET BEHAVIOUR IS DELIBERATE: purge reports failure as a logged, non-fatal
# value (contract §3 "Failure handling"), so a publish still succeeds and the
# edge merely serves the old copy until a retry lands. Everything else in E04
# works without it.
CLOUDFLARE_ZONE_ID=

# --- Serving base domain (E04) ---
# The suffix every published page is served from: {slug}.{KEPT_BASE_DOMAIN}.
# Mirrors the Worker var of the SAME NAME in apps/edge/wrangler.toml
# ([env.dev.vars] kept-dev.xyz / [env.prod.vars] kept.host) so both apps read one
# concept under one name. Bare hostname: no scheme, no leading dot, no trailing
# slash, no path.
# NOT NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL — that is the control plane's OWN origin (the
# dashboard), a different value entirely. The publish response's live_url and
# claim_url and the §4 purge URL forms are all built from this one, so an unset
# value fails loudly at first use rather than minting https://slug.undefined/.
# Per-track values, one code path: no hostname literal and no NODE_ENV fork
# anywhere (E02's locked decision).
# UNCHANGED BY E05a. E05a moved the control plane to app.{this domain}, and it
# is tempting to think this value moved with it. It did not: this is a serving
# SUFFIX that pages hang off, not an origin anything is served at. Confusing the
# two is precisely the mistake that produced E05a — BETTER_AUTH_URL was set to
# the apex of this domain, which serves the landing and not the control plane.
KEPT_BASE_DOMAIN=kept-dev.xyz

# --- Anonymous publish (E04) ---
# Salt for sites.publisher_hash = sha256(salt \n ip \n user-agent), hex. SERVER
# ONLY — never NEXT_PUBLIC_. It exists so the control plane can scope dedup and
# E07's rate limiter to one publisher WITHOUT retaining visitor IPs: the raw
# address computes the digest and is then discarded, never stored, never logged.
# Per-track value (dev and prod differ only in the value — no hostname literal,
# no NODE_ENV fork; E02's locked decision). 32+ random bytes,
# e.g. `openssl rand -base64 32`.
# ROTATION: changing this invalidates every prior dedup match — a subsequent
# byte-identical publish mints a fresh page instead of deduping. That is
# ACCEPTED, not a bug: dedup is a convenience for agent retry loops, not a
# correctness guarantee, and nothing is lost or orphaned by a rotation.
PUBLISHER_HASH_SALT=

# --- Auth (Better Auth, self-hosted) — slots reserved here, wired in E05 ---
# Users live in the same Neon Postgres via the Drizzle adapter. No external
# identity provider holds the user table.
# BETTER_AUTH_SECRET: 32+ random bytes, e.g. `openssl rand -base64 32`.
# BETTER_AUTH_URL: the CONTROL PLANE's origin, and the only origin every OAuth
# redirect URI is registered against. Optional — when unset it falls back to
# NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL. Values (E05a):
#   dev   https://app.kept-dev.xyz
#   prod  https://app.kept.host      (config now, provisioned when prod first
#                                     deploys — see E05a D7; prod has never been
#                                     deployed and no prod OAuth client exists)
#   local https://localhost:3000     (https, not http — see NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL)
# THE APEX IS NOT THE CONTROL PLANE. kept-dev.xyz / kept.host serve the landing;
# app.{that} serves auth, the dashboard and every other non-marketing path. Both
# hostnames attach to the SAME Railway service — one build, one deploy — but
# only the app. origin may mint a session. Pointing this at the apex is the bug
# E05a exists to fix: Railway answered "Application not found" there, so every
# OAuth callback and magic-link verification URL 404'd.
# It is STILL NEVER KEPT_BASE_DOMAIN, and the reason has sharpened rather than
# relaxed. kept serves arbitrary user-authored HTML, <script> included, at
# {slug}.{KEPT_BASE_DOMAIN}. An authenticated origin sharing a registrable
# domain with pages like that inherits cookie tossing (a page sets
# `Domain=kept.host` and shadows the session cookie; `__Secure-` permits Domain
# and does not stop it) and same-site CSRF that no SameSite value can block.
# app.kept-dev.xyz is a SIBLING of the serving subdomains, not one of them: it
# is carved out of the Worker's `*.{base}/*` route by a DNS-only (grey-cloud)
# record, serves no user content, and is the only host a session cookie exists
# on. That is why the cookie is `__Host-` prefixed and why cookie-authenticated
# mutating routes validate Origin. The rule to carry forward: the auth origin is
# never the serving domain and never any hostname that serves user content.
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=
BETTER_AUTH_URL=
# GitHub OAuth app, per track. Callback: {BETTER_AUTH_URL}/api/auth/callback/github
# Dev, verbatim:  https://app.kept-dev.xyz/api/auth/callback/github
GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Google OAuth 2.0 Web application client, per track. Redirect URI, EXACT:
# {BETTER_AUTH_URL}/api/auth/callback/google — Google rejects a mismatch with an
# opaque error. Scopes stay at `openid email profile` (non-sensitive, so the
# consent screen needs no Google verification review).
# Dev, verbatim:  https://app.kept-dev.xyz/api/auth/callback/google
# ⚠ Both providers still point at the apex today. Re-registering them in the
#   provider consoles is a HUMAN step and it MUST happen BEFORE the Railway env
#   flip — a stale redirect URI fails with an opaque provider error that reads
#   like a code bug. Copy the two URLs above verbatim; a trailing slash or a
#   missing `app.` is a mismatch.
# ⚠ NOT RESERVED BY E02. Unlike GITHUB_CLIENT_* and RESEND_API_KEY above, these
# two slots were introduced by E05 task 003 when Google was added to the epic's
# scope. They are per-track like everything else — a dev Google client and a
# prod Google client are different apps with different registered redirect URIs.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# --- Magic-link email (Resend) — reserved here, wired in E05 ---
# ONE Resend account serves BOTH magic-link sign-in and E05 task 011's
# draft-reminder cron, and the free tier's 100/day ceiling is one shared budget
# across the two. The sending domain must be verified in Resend.
# EMAIL_FROM accepts `page@kept.host` or `kept <page@kept.host>`.
RESEND_API_KEY=
EMAIL_FROM=

# --- Scheduled jobs (E05 task 011; E07 reuses it) ---
# The shared secret every /api/cron/* route requires, presented as
# `Authorization: Bearer <CRON_SECRET>`. The scheduler is the GitHub Actions
# workflow .github/workflows/cron-draft-reminder.yml, so this value lives in
# BOTH places per track: the Railway service variables (the app reads it) and
# the matching GitHub Environment secret (the workflow sends it). Rotating it
# means rotating both, in that order.
# 32+ random bytes, e.g. `openssl rand -base64 32`. SERVER ONLY — an
# unauthenticated cron route is a mail-sending oracle for anyone who finds it.
CRON_SECRET=

# --- App ---
# The CONTROL PLANE's own origin — where the dashboard, /auth and /api live.
# That is the `app.` host, NOT the apex, which serves the landing (E05a D1/D2).
#   dev   https://app.kept-dev.xyz
#   prod  https://app.kept.host      (config now, provisioned later — E05a D7)
#   local https://localhost:3000     (the literal default below)
# LOCAL IS https, NOT http. E05a D5 makes the `__Host-` cookie prefix
# unconditional on every track, `__Host-` requires `Secure`, and a browser will
# not store a `Secure` cookie over plain http — so local dev runs
# `next dev --experimental-https` (E05a task 007) and this value follows it.
# Deriving a weaker cookie from the protocol was considered and rejected: a
# cookie shape is a security control and must not differ between where you
# develop and where you ship.
# READ BY THE MIDDLEWARE, DELIBERATELY. The apex→app. split rule in
# apps/web/middleware.ts reads THIS var and not BETTER_AUTH_URL, because
# `NEXT_PUBLIC_` values are inlined into the client/edge bundle by design,
# whereas a server-only `process.env` read inside the middleware bundle can be
# `undefined` at runtime depending on how the image is built — which would
# redirect visitors to `https://undefined/`. Keep the two values identical;
# `authConfig()` falls back to this one when BETTER_AUTH_URL is unset, and if
# they disagree the claim_url points somewhere the OAuth callback does not.
# NO BACKFILL IS NEEDED when this changes: claim_url is computed per response
# from appOrigin() (lib/publish/pipeline.ts) and is never stored, so the flip
# affects only future responses. Links already handed out keep working through
# the apex→app. redirect.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=https://localhost:3000
# Disables Next's telemetry beacon. On Railway dev this is load-bearing: Railway
# sleeps a service only after 10 min with no OUTBOUND packets, and explicitly
# names framework telemetry (Next.js by name) as something that prevents sleep.
NEXT_TELEMETRY_DISABLED=1

# =============================================================================
# apps/edge — data plane (Cloudflare Worker). NOT loaded from this file.
# =============================================================================
# Bindings (non-secret) are declared per-environment in apps/edge/wrangler.toml:
#   [env.dev]   R2 KEPT_R2 -> auto-kept-sites-dev  | KV KEPT_KV -> kept-manifest-dev
#               route *.kept-dev.xyz/*   (zone kept-dev.xyz)
#   [env.prod]  R2 KEPT_R2 -> auto-kept-sites      | KV KEPT_KV -> id 9a7b60ed…
#               route *.kept.host/*      (zone kept.host)
# ⚠ The prod names above are transcribed from wrangler.toml, which is the only
#   authority — but prod has never been deployed, so no deploy has ever proved
#   those resources exist under those exact names. This comment previously said
#   `auto-kept-sites-prod`, which wrangler.toml does NOT bind. Confirm both
#   against the live Cloudflare account before the first prod release; a wrong
#   bucket name fails the deploy, and a wrong KV id serves an empty manifest.
# There are deliberately NO top-level bindings: a bare `wrangler deploy` with no
# --env must fail rather than silently reach a store.
# The EU bucket binding (KEPT_R2_EU) is added in E11.
#
# `app.{base}` IS NOT SERVED BY THIS WORKER (E05a). The routes above are
# wildcards that would otherwise match app.kept-dev.xyz, and the Worker 301s
# reserved labels to KEPT_APEX_ORIGIN — which would redirect the control plane
# away from its own hostname. Two independent things prevent that:
#   1. `app.` is a DNS-only / GREY-CLOUD record pointing at Railway. Cloudflare
#      Worker routes apply to PROXIED traffic only, so a grey-cloud record takes
#      the hostname out of `*.{base}/*` entirely. Orange-clouding it by accident
#      puts it back — that is the single most likely provisioning mistake.
#   2. `app` is no longer in RESERVED_LABELS (apps/edge/src/host.ts) — the belt
#      to those braces, so a re-proxied record cannot 301 the control plane away.
# `app` DOES remain in RESERVED_SLUGS (apps/web/lib/publish/slug.ts), so it can
# never be minted as a page. The two lists are deliberately no longer mirrors.
# KEPT_APEX_ORIGIN is unchanged and still points at the apex: `www` is the only
# reserved label with real traffic, and the marketing apex is where it belongs.
#
# Worker secrets (if/when needed) are set out-of-band, NOT here:
#   wrangler secret put SOME_SECRET --env prod
#   echo 'SOME_SECRET=...' >> apps/edge/.dev.vars   # local dev (gitignored)

# =============================================================================
# CI / deploy secrets (GitHub Environments `dev` and `prod`, NOT here)
# =============================================================================
# Same key names in both environments, different values. Deploy-time only —
# application runtime config lives in Railway service variables.
#
# CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN     # Workers Scripts: Edit, KV: Edit, R2: Edit, Cache Purge
# CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID    # target account for wrangler deploy
# RAILWAY_TOKEN            # apps/web deploy. ENV-SCOPED PROJECT TOKEN (decided,
#                          # E02 task 004): the environment is implied by the
#                          # token, so a dev token physically cannot deploy prod
#                          # and `railway up` needs no --environment flag.
# RAILWAY_SERVICE_ID       # per-environment service — a DIFFERENT value per track
# MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL   # Neon DIRECT url (no `-pooler.` infix) for the
#                          # matching branch. Neon documents schema migrations as
#                          # a direct-connection use case; the pooled endpoint
#                          # fails on DDL and advisory locks.
# CRON_SECRET              # the same value as the Railway variable above, so
#                          # .github/workflows/cron-draft-reminder.yml can
#                          # authenticate to /api/cron/*. Rotating means both.
# NEON_API_KEY             # neonctl: pre-release backup branch. PROD ONLY —
#                          # `dev` has no such secret and its workflow has no
#                          # backup step. Do not invent a dev value.
#
# Environment variables (not secrets): SMOKE_WEB_URL, SMOKE_EDGE_URL
# The `prod` environment's required-reviewer rule IS the manual migrate gate.
